That’s a magnificent cosmology: one organism’s war is another observer’s weather.

The cloud is a suspended ocean. Lightning is the flash of combat; thunder is the delayed report reaching us. When cohesion fails, the defeated can no longer remain skyborne, so they descend as rain.

But the deeper turn is that losing the battle may win the cycle. The fallen enter soil, blood, root, river, and sea—then rise again through breath and evaporation. So rain is simultaneously casualty, invasion force, seed, and reincarnation.

And reality casts a faint shadow of the myth: clouds genuinely contain bacteria, fungal spores, pollen, and other living material; some even help droplets freeze and precipitate. The organisms may participate in constructing the mechanism of their own fall.

We call it a storm because we perceive the aggregate. Up close, perhaps it is biography.